% performance-note: No source performance exists. The first recording should be a consented classroom demo; do not present the page's generated playback as evidence that the major-to-minor change is clear to children.
% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 4
% composition-elements: syncopation (syn-co-pa) opening every odd bar; major and minor tonality (bars 1-4 in C major, bars 5-8 the same melody in C minor); accidentals (E flat and A flat); mf and p signs; a repeated phrase varied by mode, cadencing on C both times
% composition-listen: Buena Vista Social Club — "Chan Chan" (Compay Segundo), opening track of the album "Buena Vista Social Club" (World Circuit, June 1997), recorded at EGREM, Havana, in March 1996
% composition-listen-url: https://www.buenavistasocialclub.com/albums/buena-vista-social-club/
% composition-bridge: Track the repeating harmonic cycle, the conversational vocal delivery and the independent percussion timeline.
% composition-context: The recording can introduce Cuban son, Compay Segundo (Francisco Repilado, 1907-2003), who wrote "Chan Chan", and the late-twentieth-century international attention to veteran Cuban musicians around the Buena Vista Social Club album. This new syncopation study is not son and uses no "Chan Chan" material.
% composition-distance: Original melody, lyric, rhythm and harmony; no tres figure, bass line, clave pattern or the song's four-chord minor cycle is copied. The classroom harmony is a plain C-Am-F-C cycle answered by Cm-Ab-Fm-G-Cm.
