% dossier-function: original Grade 4 song and listening bridge for syncopation and major/minor contrast; the class sings a lantern phrase in C major, then the same notes in C minor, before listening for son's repeating cycle and its singer-and-chorus conversation in "Chan Chan"
% dossier-elements: eight bars in 4/4. Bars 1-4 (C major): C4 eighth, D4 quarter, E4 eighth (syn-co-pa), G4-G4-A4-G4 eighths | A4-G4 quarters, E4 half | syn-co-pa C4-D4-E4, G4-A4-C5-A4 eighths | G4-E4 quarters, C4 half. Bars 5-8 repeat the rhythm and contour in C minor with E flat and A flat, ending G4-D4 quarters and C4 half over G to Cm. Pitch set C-D-E-G-A in the major half, C-D-E flat-G-A flat in the minor half; written range C4-C5; eighths, quarters, halves; mf and p
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 92 is the target; begin at 76, speaking "a LAN-tern" as syn-co-pa over a steady beat before singing. C major (and its parallel C minor, with E flat and A flat written as accidentals) is the rehearsal key; the written range C4-C5 sits inside the young-unison C4-D5 range and the Grade 4 voice range. Teach in steps: chant the rhythm, sing the major half, then sing the minor half and ask what changed; name the two altered notes on the staff
% dossier-accompaniment: The page's folk style (guitar boom-chick and bass) plays the written chords, C-Am-C/F-C then Cm-Ab-Cm/Fm-G/Cm; it is a neutral school accompaniment, not son, and must not be labelled son, salsa or Cuban. Do not add a clave, tres montuno or bongó pattern to make it sound Cuban
% dossier-lyrics: Original English words written for this record: "A lantern in the window, warm and bright, a lantern by the doorway lights the night. A lantern in the shadow, dim and low, a lantern on the pathway leads us home." One syllable per note; the syncopated quarter carries the stressed syllable (a LAN-tern)
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original course composition made for Grand Central Stationery on 11 November 2026 under human-directed range, grade and form constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community and not modelled on the melody, harmony or words of "Chan Chan"; the recording is a listening bridge only
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the melody, words, chords and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. "Chan Chan" and the Buena Vista Social Club recording remain the copyright of their owners and are not included
% dossier-respect: Identify Cuban son as an Afro-Cuban and Spanish-descended tradition from eastern Cuba, name Compay Segundo and the musicians on the recording (among them Eliades Ochoa, Ibrahim Ferrer and Omara Portuondo), and note that the album's fame outside Cuba came through a foreign-produced project. Do not call every clave-adjacent classroom pattern salsa or son, and never present this piece as Cuban music
% dossier-review: Cuban son educator, Grade 4 teacher and second score reader; all reviews still required
