N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals eight eighths with no pickup. Bar 1 C4 eighth, D4 quarter, E4 eighth, G4-G4-A4-G4 eighths; bar 2 A4 and G4 quarters, E4 half; bar 3 C4 eighth, D4 quarter, E4 eighth, G4-A4-C5-A4 eighths; bar 4 G4 and E4 quarters, C4 half. Bars 5-8 repeat bars 1-4 with E flat for E and A flat for A, and bar 8 ends G4-D4 quarters and a C4 half. Four syncopations, each a quarter on the off-beat of beat 1 (bars 1, 3, 5, 7); no dotted notes, sixteenths or ties. Range C4-C5, largest leap a fourth (G4 down to D4 in bar 8); the rest moves by steps and thirds. Chords: C | Am | C F | C || Cm | Ab | Cm Fm | G Cm, each fitting the melody's beat notes. Form: one four-bar phrase in major and its minor variant, both cadencing on C. Originality check: compared with "Chan Chan" (its four-chord minor cycle, tres figure and melody are not used) and with common syncopated school songs and folk tunes (Mango Walk, Day-O, Tingalayo, Hush Little Baby, This Little Light of Mine); the C-D-E syn-co-pa opening is a common cell and the phrase matches none of them.
N:UNCERTAIN: The analyzer reads the chord symbols as Grade 7 harmony (minor-mode and borrowed chords); the course's own grading leaves chord texture out because the Band, not the students, plays them, and a teacher should confirm that the melody is Grade 4. Whether children hear the switch to minor without a cue, and whether 92 is a comfortable target, need a classroom trial; the comparison with son needs a Cuban son educator.
